g  something like the banana bread  won't cause bad things to happen or that  she's just eating intuitively when she  chooses something  that diet culture would deem as bad so  this tells me  that even though she is trialling a diet  that's intended purposes by the original  creator  was for weight loss blair isn't doing it  to lose weight she's making a sassy  cultural statement  about the sensationalism of these  outrageous  unsustainable and unbalanced diets that  we often find online  it's not unlike what i do but from the  perspective of a viewer  like you who might be tempted to try it  rather than from a nutritional  professional  having said that of course if you're  just watching a blair walnut's video  here or there  or you're looking at her click bait pro  skinny titles  i can see why it can be confusing or  even triggering to some viewers  now from my perspective after watching  blair's own what i in a day videos  it doesn't look like she herself follows  any of these restrictive  or carefully controlled diets if she did  i would think that she wouldn't have the  emotional flexibility to be able to jump  into each of these insane challenges  without panicking  what the impact would be of eating an  excess or in a deficit for a single day  she's smart she knows it's just for  funsies  and that her body will kind of figure  itself out in the days to follow  i actually think that it takes someone  with a really strong faith in their body  and a weight neutral mentality to be  able to kind of waffle between some of  these outrageous youtuber  and celebrity diets and then to eat  moderately on their own  now aside from following people's diets  blaire has also tried some popular  fad diets like intermittent fasting and  the keto diet  in her video on intermittent fasting  where she followed the diet of tick-tock  star dixie dimelio  she lists the benefits of intermittent  fasting including weight loss  blood sugar regulation brain functioning  skin health  reductions in inflammation and risk of  diabetes while i do appreciate blair  highlighting that intermittent fasting  is  not just about weight loss since that is  the primary narrative in the media  whether or not all of these claims of  blares are evidence-based  is up for debate i know you guys want me  to do a whole video on instrument  fasting and  i do need to because there is a lot to  cover  but let me quickly summarize based on  these quick claims that she made  yes there is some research to support  the health claims of intermittent  fasting  but the problem is that a lot of these  claims are based on  animal studies and really short-term  studies so  we do need more long-term studies to  confirm its benefits  and the sustainability for long-term  weight loss for example  a 2016 review showed that while most  individuals following intermittent  fasting  did lose weight most of the weight  happened really early on in the study  and plateaued around the six-month mark  with the rate of any subsequent weight  loss  slowing down around the one to two year  mark  there is also some research to support  the benefits of intermittent fasting for  inflammation  as well as blood sugar management so  that's not untrue  as for the benefits of intermittent  fasting and cognitive functions  well animal studies and anecdotal  reports have shown promising results  similar to what blair experienced for  herself  when she said that it enhanced her mood  however we  don't have a lot of strong human  evidence to back this one up yet  another popular fad diet that blair  tried on her channel  was keto which happened to be sponsored  by aikido app  now normally i would totally call that  out as shady like i did with alyssa  when she did this quick shift from  veganism to carnivore  in line with her butcher box sponsor but  because trialing these popular diets is  kind of blair's format  and she didn't claim that she was going  to do keto for life  i guess it makes a little bit mo