ee bowls of soup in one sitting  that's a lot of water volume so while it  may temporarily make you feel  full maybe even uncomfortably full from  the water  and salt you'll probably start to feel  hunger creep up  later on and that can be really tricky  if you're feeling hungry  but you also feel a little bloated and  uncomfortable at the same time  so i would say that taking the time to  sit down to an  additional midday meal or even a  substantial  fast snack earlier on in the day might  allow for more comfortable portions  by the time that dinner comes lastly  let's take a look at i'm going dessert  start on my nightly ice cream  now i like all sorts of ice cream oreo  ice cream chocolate ice cream i just  don't like vanilla ice cream because i  don't like to be vanilla  and boring but i did have this new ice  cream today which is strawberries and  cream and it was  very delicious i also noticed i eat my  ice cream like a serial killer  very rhythmically precisely and in a  circle like am i okay  i think not but i'm sure if i ate like  another bowl of soup i would have eaten  less ice cream ice cream is more fun to  eat and i like to just eat what i like  to eat  and i don't force myself to eat things  just because they're healthy and today i  felt like having three desserts after  dinner and that's what i did and  nobody not even jesus or the police can  stop me from having three desserts  walnut reporting i just realized i ate  an entire bag of popcorn  and forgot to drink water all day which  is probably the reason i just ate a  bunch of sweets and  like had four desserts  um so i would like to call jesus and  ask him to turn this water into wine  because i don't really want water right  now i want wine so  okay so she's totally right no one not  the police not jesus  not abby sharp can stop anyone from  choosing to have  three desserts if they feel like it and  no i'm not comparing myself to jesus or  the police for that matter definitely  not  i'm just rolling with her joke here but  honestly i know that this is  a lot of fun food but this actually does  seem  to be intuitive to me blair had a  particularly  light day of eating and i don't think  that it was planned that way  i mean sometimes we just have really  busy days and  we don't hear our hunger cues as clearly  as usual  so we skip meals and by the time that we  sit down  and relax we are ravenous so if blaire  were to force herself to eat a fourth  bowl of soup in an effort to curtail her  desire for dessert  i would actually argue that that  wouldn't be as intuitive  as having three post-dinner snacks so  while blair had a pack of oreo ice cream  a serving of strawberry ice cream and a  bag of popcorn  i have a feeling that she was just  responding to the hunger  that had kind of mounted over the day of  not eating to her needs  not only did she maybe forget to eat  enough during the day but she also  seemed to forget to drink enough  water which she said is probably one of  the reasons why she was  so ravenous for sweets now nutrition  wise of course  this snack sesh is high in sugar  saturated fat  and salt there's no question it's also  very likely  based on my professional experience as a  dietitian that having more fuel spaced  out throughout the day  wouldn't have left blair needing to have  three bowls of the same soup at dinner  and still hungry for three desserts  afterwards  she may have been satiated after just  one bowl of soup  or one dessert but blaire was simply  responding  to her body's cues when she had the  opportunity  to actually listen to them and not  surprisingly  her body is asking for more food even if  that food  isn't the most nutritionally balanced  sure i could tell blair to have  one ice cream with maybe a piece of  peanut butter toast and an  egg or a yogurt parfait like she did for  breakfast on day two  that would certainly be a lot more  balanced of a snack  that would probably feel better to most  people who are watching  but the important thing is is that blair  didn't judge herself  for these snack choices she knew that  she was just unusually hungry because  the decisions that she made earlier on  in the day  and she just moved on that's awesome a  lot of people critique intuitive eating  thinking that it's  just this meal right here eating  desserts  just because you want to but it's not  it's a journey  it's a process that involves collecting  data  every single day data that will inform  your future decisions  if you choose to let it so perhaps blair  would take  this day and say to herself hey i got  busy  i forgot to have lunch i forgot to drink  water and the result was that i ended up  eating three bowls of soup  and then still being hungry and eating  three desserts  maybe this felt fine to her i mean she's  a lot younger than me  but over time this pattern of eating  might start to impact  digestion energy blood sugars and more  and it probably wouldn't feel so good  being an intuitive eater doesn't mean  that you always perfectly listen to your  hunger and satiety cues  or that you always respond to the data  that you've gathered  but what it does mean is that whatever  happens  you move on you give yourself permission  to eat  however you have to eat in the  circumstances  without guilt shame or further  restriction  you say hey i let myself get too hungry  and thirsty  and i ate three desserts whatever  tomorrow is another  delicious day my sense is that  blaire just inherently gets this and  that's  more important to me to see than how  balanced her bedtime snack was one day  so let's move on to our big questions in  general  is this way of eating balanced well i  really love blair's easy going and  relaxed approach to food  she eats based on what she likes and  what she has in the fridge  and what makes her feel good in that  moment she also doesn't seem to follow  any strict food rules or restrictions  and isn't concerned about eating healthy  all the time which  is always really refreshing right now on  youtube  in one video where she tried to make  chrissy teigen's famous banana bread  and epically failed she goes on to say  that  eating banana bread is not a big deal it  will not make you gain weight  and that nothing bad will happen other  than maybe  you'll make banana bread every day and  then there will be a worldwide banana  shortage  and that would be bad news bears for  someone we know so  needless to say i love her laid back  approach to food  with that said from a nutrition  perspective there are a few gaps that i  think we want to address  now depending on the day blair is  typically consuming between  2000 and 2200 calories a day which would  be aligned with her caloric needs  with the exception of day two which was  a little skimpy at  1300 calories now i can make a big stink  about this but  i want you to know that it's completely  normal to not eat the exact same amount  of food every day  if you're eating intuitively so it's  expected that the total number of  calories that we consume  may fluctuate day to day based on our  hunger cues  stress levels physical activity sleep  etc  maybe she ate like one of our cheat day  youtubers the day before so her body was  kind of recalibrating  or maybe she was just really busy that  day and the next day when she had more  time  she caught up and ate more unfortunately  i don't have access to her daily food  log but  based on the attitude i see from her  videos  i think there's a good chance that blair  would just kind of let her body  take the lead now when it comes to her  total macronutrient intake  she's consuming a moderate carb and  protein diet  with some days getting a higher  proportion of calories from fat  this is likely because some days she's  consuming almost double the recommended  saturated fat intake from foods like  steak  ice cream and cheese which may or may  not be a problem for  health i did a whole video on the  controversial research on saturated fat  but to summarize some research says that  it may increase risk factors of heart  disease  and other research claims that has no  effect on health  at all but as dietitians because the  research on saturated fat  is so inconclusive we still like to err  on the side of caution and aim for  greater intakes of healthy unsaturated  fats  and complex carbohydrates instead which  do have  demonstrated benefits on heart health  this includes foods like  nuts seeds avocado fatty fish and olive  oil for fats  and whole grains sweet potatoes and  berries for carbs for example  so for me it's less about cutting high  fat foods out of the diet  and instead focusing on what we can add  in  the rest often works itself out and on  the topic of adding more complex carbs  this would also help blare me her fiber  needs since on some days  she's only getting between 14 and 16  grams which is below the 25 gram minimum  recommendation so that might mean adding  a little bit more fruit at breakfast and  snacks  veggies with her meals and some whole  grains throughout the day  i did hear her say that she tries to eat  her you know four or five servings of  fruit a day to keep her regular  so even though i didn't see much aside  from that breakfast  i have to just assume that no two days  are alike for blair  and it's possible that these aren't  really good examples of her usual  routine  i also know that we're in quarantine  which can make getting fresh fruit and  veggies  a little more challenging but like i  said there's nothing wrong with throwing  in some frozen canned or even dried  fruit and veg  lastly blair had also mentioned in one  of her videos that her iron levels are  low  and that she's anemic and therefore  needs to eat a lot of meat  and spinach totally fair anemia is no  joke and  even with the meat in there our analysis  suggests that blair's intake  is still below the 18 milligram  recommendation  for people who menstruate it's actually  somewhere between 8  and 12 milligrams per day so if she  wants to upper intake without the  saturated fat found in a lot of red meat  she could also add in more tofu or lean  poultry  which we did see in her diet so that's  great plus some lentils beans  pumpkin seeds baked potatoes or dried  apricots  for the plant-based sources she can  maximize the iron absorption of these  foods by pairing it with a source of  vitamin c  so an example of this could be something  like a trail mix with pumpkin seeds  and dried berries for a balanced snack  or maybe lentil soup with a lot of lemon  and squash in there or maybe a stir fry  with some tofu  and bell peppers and broccoli now are  there any problematic claims or  assumptions made by this channel  as i mentioned earlier blair is known  for trying out people's diets  usually showing some kind of weight loss  results and giving her overall opinion  on the specific diet blaire will often  title her videos like how to be skinny  or a skinny legend  or how to lose weight fast which is  usually paired with  a before and after weight loss photo in  the thumbnail at first glance  this combination of weight loss imagery  and seemingly  positive appraisal of being skinny like  some  famous celebrity seems to just  perpetuate diet culture  fat phobia and even some disordered  eating behaviors  however if you actually watch enough of  blair  and i've been watching because this girl  is really entertaining  you'll actually start to pick up on the  satirical  nature of her content it's kind of like  those  jp sears videos that poke fun at  wellness culture trends like  youtubers going on vegan when you  actually  watch her and the moments of realism in  her videos  you'll see some evidence of her true  thoughts for example  in her video on gigi hadid's diet after  weighing herself before the diet  she literally says that weight does not  matter  and it's just a number like your social  security number or your street number  she also often talks about how eating  something like the banana bread  won't cause bad things to happen or that  she's just eating intuitively when she  chooses something  that diet culture would deem as bad so  this tells me  that even though she is trialling a diet  that's intended purposes by the original  creator  was for weight loss blair isn't doing it  to lose weight she's making a sassy  cultural statement  about the sensationalism of these  outrageous  unsustainable and unbalanced diets that  we often find online  it's not unlike what i do but from the  perspective of a viewer  like you who might be tempted to try it  rather than from a nutritional  professional  having said that of course if you're  just watching a blair walnut's video  here or there  or you're looking at her click bait pro  skinny titles  i can see why it can be confusing or  even triggering to some viewers  now from my perspective after watching  blair's own what i in a day videos  it doesn't look like she herself follows  any of these restrictive  or carefully controlled diets if she did  i would think that she wouldn't have the  emotional flexibility to be able to jump  into each of these insane challenges  without panicking  what the impact would be of eating an  excess or in a deficit for a single day  she's smart she knows it's just for  funsies  and that her body will kind of figure  itself out in the days to follow  i actually think that it takes someone  with a really strong faith in their body  and a weight neutral mentality to be  able to kind of waffle between some of  these outrageous youtuber  and celebrity diets and then to eat  moderately on their own  now aside from following people's diets  blaire has also tried some popular  fad diets like intermittent fasting and  the keto diet  in her video on intermittent fasting  where she followed the diet of tick-tock  star dixie dimelio  she lists the benefits of intermittent  fasting including weight loss  blood sugar regulation brain functioning  skin health  reductions in inflammation and risk of  diabetes while i do appreciate blair  highlighting that intermittent fasting  is  not just about weight loss since that is  the primary narrative in the media  whether or not all of these claims of  blares are evidence-based  is up for debate i know you guys want me  to do a whole video on instrument  fasting and  i do need to because there is a lot to  cover  but let me quickly summarize based on  these quick claims that she made  yes there is some research to support  the health claims of intermittent  fasting  but the problem is that a lot of these  claims are based on  animal studies and really short-term  studies so  we do need more long-term studies to  confirm its benefits  and the sustainability for long-term  weight loss for example  a 2016 review showed that while most  individuals following intermittent  fasting  did lose weight most of the weight  happened really early on in the study  and plateaued around the six-month mark  with the rate of any subsequent weight  loss  slowing down around the one to two year  mark  there is also some research to support  the benefits of intermittent fasting for  inflammation  as well as blood sugar management so  that's not untrue  as for the benefits of intermittent  fasting and cognitive functions  well animal studies and anecdotal  reports have shown promising results  similar to what blair experienced for  herself  when she said that it enhanced her mood  however we  don't have a lot of strong human  evidence to back this one up yet  another popular fad diet that blair  tried on her channel  was keto which happened to be sponsored  by aikido app  now normally i would totally call that  out as shady like i did with alyssa  when she did this quick shift from  veganism to carnivore  in line with her butcher box sponsor but  because trialing these popular diets is  kind of blair's format  and she didn't claim that she was going  to do keto for life  i guess it makes a little bit more sense  anyways  in this video she ate some pretty  nutritionally dense meals like  shakshuka for breakfast salmon and  radishes for lunch  and some chicken and asparagus for  dinner this is a far  cry from the cheese on bacon on beef  diet  that we often see characterized as keto  online so  i am glad that she was able to find a  version of the diet  that felt good to her what i liked about  this diet experiment  is that she used a lot of positive  language when describing food  and the keto diet in general for  instance she talked about liking salmon  because it has  more texture and flavor which makes it  extra yummy  and that the keto diet helped her to  feel really satiated so that she didn't  feel the need to snack as much  this impact on appetite on the keto diet  is actually quite common  as studies have shown that ketosis can  suppress the hunger hormone ghrelin  causing a reduction in appetite so this  could explain why some people feel like  they have success on keto  because fat is just inherently very  satiating  as for the benefits of the keto diet  blaire mentions that while the keto diet  can promote weight loss  it can also lower cholesterol improve  blood sugar levels  and regulate appetite and blood pressure  if you missed my older video on keto you  can definitely check it out right here  but in short i would say that these  claims about the keto diet that blair  mentions  are largely supported by the literature  when it comes to heart health  the research on keto is a little bit  mixed  some research suggests that over time  the keto diet may have  a negative impact on cholesterol levels  while other research has found  improvements in cholesterol levels and  other risk factors for heart disease  like blood pressure  studies have also found that keto may  help to lower blood sugar levels  in people with insulin resistance and  type 2 diabetes but  that it's not of course appropriate for  people with type 1 diabetes  due to the risk of hypoglycemia having  said  all of that these diets are not benign  both come with serious risks and while  it may be fun to play along for a day or  a week  it gives us no information how well one  might do  or fare in the long run and we know that  for the vast majority of people  any restrictive diet is typically poorly  sustained  so since blair's content seems to be  about entertainment anyway  i would say that if she's going to touch  on the alleged benefits  of a diet that she's trying out i also  think it's only fair that she also  outlines  the potential risks and who it may not  be for  finally what can we take away that we  love about blair's channel  well i think blair is super entertaining  and hilarious to watch so  i can totally understand the popularity  of her channel  when it comes to her actual recipes i  appreciate that unlike a lot of  lifestyle vloggers  blair's recipes are really realistic  really accessible  and definitely not deliberately  instagramable  she eats what she has on hand and what  she likes and that varies from day to  day which  is so refreshing to see she also seems  to be morally neutral when it comes to  weight  and food and while she may play the  typical  diet debbie following along with the  latest food fads in her real life  she doesn't seem to follow any of these  crazy restrictive food rules that she  mocks on the daily bottom line if you're  looking for some fun  really hilarious youtube content  definitely consider blair's channel  of course her channel may not be for  everyone especially  if weight loss language and imagery is  triggering for you  or if you're easily offended by  satirical humor  but overall her content is pretty  light-hearted  fun and more of a mockery on dye culture  than an endorsement of it so i'm all for  poking fun at die culture in a hilarious  way so  kudos to blair for finding a clever way  to do just that  and that is all for today folks a big  thank you again to spotlight for  sponsoring this video  if you liked it be sure to give it the  thumbs up leave me a comment below with  any youtuber that you want me to review  subscribe to the channel and i will see  you next time on abby's kitchen  bye  you
 

